<header><p class="st-kicker">county · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Zutphen</h1><p class="st-meta">1046 CE – 1138 CE (approx.) · capital Zutphen</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A short-lived county on the IJssel that merged with Guelders in the 12th century and gave its name to one of the duchy's four quarters.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The county of Zutphen took shape in the mid-11th century around the trading town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ijssel">IJssel</a>. Its early counts held lands granted by the German kings and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-utrecht">Utrecht</a>, but details of its first rulers are scarce.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Zutphen was a river-trade centre on the route between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zuiderzee">Zuiderzee</a>, and the county included rich farmland in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achterhoek">Achterhoek</a>. Its counts were linked by marriage to the leading families of the region.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 1138 the heiress Ermgard of Zutphen's marriage to Gerard of Wassenberg, count of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-guelders">Guelders</a>, united Zutphen with the County of Guelders. It became one of the four quarters of the later Duchy of Guelders, and the town of Zutphen joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-zutphen.jpg" alt="County of Zutphen" width="320" height="410"><figcaption>Image: Sir Iain · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Locator_County_of_Zutphen_(1350).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1046 CE – 1138 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Zutphen</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ermgard of Zutphen</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Dutch</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/netherlands">Netherlands</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Zutphen</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1092&amp;focus=county-of-zutphen" aria-label="County of Zutphen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4819 377 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Zutphen lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5139 633L5229 694M5139 633L5143 650"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5143 650h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5139 633h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5139 633h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Zutphen. <a href="/atlas?year=1092&amp;focus=county-of-zutphen">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
